    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d378">

                <pb facs="1598" n="1536" sameAs="frus:frus1945v01/pg1536"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.C115 Paris/7–2045: Airgram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in Belgium</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Sawyer</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-08-07">August 7, 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>A–165. With reference to Despatch [<hi rendition="#italic">Airgram</hi>] A–239,
                    July 11, this Government would welcome the renewal of Soviet membership in the
                    International Labor Organization.</p>
                <p>This position has been evidenced on several occasions. At the December 1943
                    meeting of the Governing Body of the ILO at London<anchor n="12"
                        xml:id="d378-f1"/> the United States representative supported the motion to
                    invite the USSR to participate as a member in the 26th Session.<anchor n="13"
                        xml:id="d378-f2"/> The American member of the Workers’ Group and of the
                    Employers’ Group, respectively, were present at that meeting. The action was
                    unanimous. At the June 1945 meeting at Quebec the previous action, which had
                    been taken at a secret session, was put in the public record by the Workers’
                    member from the United States, Robert J. Watt, who is an
                    officer of the American Federation of Labor.<anchor n="14" xml:id="d378-f3"/> He
                    advises Department that A. F. of L. favors Soviet participation in ILO and other
                    intergovernmental organizations.</p>
                <p>American members joined in Governing Body invitation at Quebec to members of the
                    United Nations which are not now members of the ILO to send observers to the
                    27th Session of the International Labor Conference.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d378-f1" n="12" xml:id="d378-f1-content">
                <p>Ninety-first session of the Governing Body of the ILO, London, December 16–20,
                    1943; for account of this meeting, see <hi rendition="#italic">International
                        Labor Review</hi>, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">xlix</hi>, March, 1944,
                    pp. 347–351; see also <ref target="frus1944v02#pg_1007">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1944, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, pp. 1007</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d378-f2" n="13" xml:id="d378-f2-content">
                <p>See telegram 8835, December 19, 1943, from London, <ref
                        target="frus1944v02#pg_1013">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, p. 1013</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d378-f3" n="14" xml:id="d378-f3-content">
                <p>Mr. Watt was International Representative of the American
                    Federation of Labor.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
